WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.769
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy and Grammy

00:00:02.769 --> 00:00:04.770
nominated TV
and record producer,

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:07.306
you know, from shows such as
"The X Factor,"

00:00:07.306 --> 00:00:09.275
"American Idol,"
and "America's Got Talent."

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:12.178
His new show,
"America's Got Talent Extreme,"

00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:14.347
airs Mondays at 8:00
right here on NBC.

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:15.915
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.915 --> 00:00:18.184
-[Bleep]

00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:19.686
-Oh, God!

00:00:19.686 --> 00:00:21.754
-♪ Tricky, tricky, tricky ♪

00:00:21.754 --> 00:00:24.024
[ Audience screams ]

00:00:27.026 --> 00:00:28.962
-Oh, no too far, man!

00:00:28.962 --> 00:00:30.663
[ Music climbs ]

00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:36.068
-♪ It's like that job
that we don't quit ♪

00:00:36.068 --> 00:00:38.038
-With a drift.
That drift was for you, Travis.

00:00:40.473 --> 00:00:43.643
-Please welcome to the show,
Simon Cowell. Hello, Simon.

00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:47.513
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Hello, Seth.

00:00:47.513 --> 00:00:51.017
-Simon, I'm just going to start
by saying this.

00:00:51.017 --> 00:00:54.721
That might be the best clip
we've ever shown to introduce

00:00:54.721 --> 00:00:56.455
a guest
in the history of this show.

00:00:56.455 --> 00:01:01.161
-Thank you. I mean, that was
that was a wild show.

00:01:01.161 --> 00:01:02.561
-Yeah.

00:01:02.561 --> 00:01:05.931
This is a I do want to start
by asking how you're doing.

00:01:05.931 --> 00:01:07.166
You have a broken arm

00:01:07.166 --> 00:01:11.104
and I I believe
you broke this bike riding.

00:01:11.104 --> 00:01:13.239
Is that correct?

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:16.209
-It's not as bad as your hand.
-Yeah, no, exactly.

00:01:16.209 --> 00:01:17.510
I mean, that thing still shows

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:21.781
and I think
it's not coming off the makeup.

00:01:21.781 --> 00:01:25.051
But you broke your back
bike riding in 2020, as well.

00:01:25.051 --> 00:01:28.488
Now are you?
Are you bad at biking?

00:01:28.488 --> 00:01:31.724
[ Laughter ]

00:01:31.724 --> 00:01:33.893
-Well, yes, actually.

00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:36.095
I mean, yeah,

00:01:36.095 --> 00:01:39.498
for that to happen,
what was it within like a year?

00:01:39.498 --> 00:01:41.434
Yeah, it wasn't great.

00:01:41.434 --> 00:01:45.538
The irony was, is that part
of my kind of like physio

00:01:45.538 --> 00:01:52.211
was to cycle,
so it didn't quite work out.

00:01:52.211 --> 00:01:55.548
So this is going to be on
for about a month or so

00:01:55.548 --> 00:01:58.618
and there are literally
will be back on my bike again.

00:01:58.618 --> 00:02:00.119
-Well, that's I mean, this
speaks to

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:01.187
your commitment to cycling.

00:02:01.187 --> 00:02:03.256
There's nothing,
it seems at this point,

00:02:03.256 --> 00:02:06.325
there's nothing that
will keep you off your bike.

00:02:06.325 --> 00:02:07.693
-No.
-Yeah.

00:02:07.693 --> 00:02:10.396
-I do love it, I'm just not very
good at it, like you said.

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:14.834
-Now this show your fellow
judges -- you have a WWE star,

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:18.037
you have a motocross
professional, and yourself --

00:02:18.037 --> 00:02:21.407
Is it weird that
you're the one in a cast?

00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:23.109
[ Laughter ]

00:02:23.109 --> 00:02:25.744
-I'm not the only one,
actually, Travis,

00:02:25.744 --> 00:02:28.281
who's, you know, like you said,
one of the other judges,

00:02:28.281 --> 00:02:32.184
a week before me was jumping off
a building or something.

00:02:32.184 --> 00:02:35.288
The parachute didn't open
so he broke his pelvis.

00:02:35.288 --> 00:02:37.657
Then a few days later,
I broke my arm.

00:02:37.657 --> 00:02:39.859
So I sent him a note saying,

00:02:39.859 --> 00:02:43.095
"We're both in is like, you
know, we've got broken bones.

00:02:43.095 --> 00:02:44.863
You've just got
the better story."

00:02:44.863 --> 00:02:47.233
-Yeah, yeah, definitely on set.

00:02:47.233 --> 00:02:49.168
You want to make sure
you tell yours first.

00:02:49.168 --> 00:02:51.170
You don't want to,
you don't want to follow

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:54.073
the parachute
didn't open story.

00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:56.042
-But there is a kind of an irony
on this show

00:02:56.042 --> 00:02:58.411
that two out of the three
judges, like you said,

00:02:58.411 --> 00:03:01.180
are watching the show
with broken bones right now.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:04.483
-Do you ever, do you -- when you
watch these extreme stunts

00:03:04.483 --> 00:03:06.719
and meet these extreme
sport athletes,

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:11.824
do you ever feel the pull
to try what they like to do?

00:03:11.824 --> 00:03:13.225
-A million percent.

00:03:13.225 --> 00:03:17.029
There was one guy who --

00:03:17.029 --> 00:03:21.500
Yeah, he was on episode one,
shot himself out of a cannon

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.235
and he went about
100 feet in the air

00:03:23.235 --> 00:03:25.304
through this flaming ring.

00:03:25.304 --> 00:03:28.541
And I was thinking
if I hadn't broken my back,

00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:31.577
I think I would have
done that one.

00:03:31.577 --> 00:03:33.279
-Well, then I can't believe
I'm saying this.

00:03:33.279 --> 00:03:35.114
Breaking your back
might have been the best thing

00:03:35.114 --> 00:03:37.950
that ever happened to you.
-Exactly.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.085
there's always a silver lining.

00:03:40.085 --> 00:03:43.489
-Now, you know, I'm wondering
as you sort of branch out,

00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:46.525
certainly into "America's
Got Talent" as a world.

00:03:46.525 --> 00:03:47.860
You know, I would imagine
for a long time people

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:49.094
thought you could
only judge singing.

00:03:49.094 --> 00:03:50.529
And then now
you judge everything.

00:03:50.529 --> 00:03:56.101
Do people on the streets
ever ask you to judge a talent?

00:03:56.101 --> 00:03:57.303
-Yes, Seth.
-They do?

00:03:57.303 --> 00:03:59.805
-I get it. Yeah.

00:03:59.805 --> 00:04:04.343
And sometimes
very uncomfortable situations.

00:04:04.343 --> 00:04:06.011
I mean.

00:04:06.011 --> 00:04:09.882
The most famous, I think,
being -- this is a true story --

00:04:09.882 --> 00:04:12.452
A couple came up to me
in a restaurant

00:04:12.452 --> 00:04:15.854
and said we'll pay you
X amount of dollars

00:04:15.854 --> 00:04:19.858
to watch them having sex
and will I judge it?

00:04:19.858 --> 00:04:25.531
And I said, "Well, how much?"
And stupidly said no.

00:04:25.531 --> 00:04:28.501
If you're watching.
I'll do it.

00:04:28.501 --> 00:04:30.770
[ Laughter ]

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:33.973
-Simon, I got a look,
you can say no.

00:04:33.973 --> 00:04:35.574
What was it?
Well, it's X amount of dollars.

00:04:35.574 --> 00:04:37.610
What's the number
they throw out?

00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:45.784
-I think from memory, I mean --
I think it was about 150 grand.

00:04:45.784 --> 00:04:50.723
-Wow! That's a weird number.
-Yeah.

00:04:50.723 --> 00:04:52.525
I mean, I don't know
how they calculated it.

00:04:52.525 --> 00:04:54.093
-Yeah.

00:04:54.093 --> 00:04:55.828
I wonder if they were like,
"Should we do $100,000?"

00:04:55.828 --> 00:04:57.930
"That's not enough."
"$200,000?" "Don't be crazy."

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:02.001
[ Laughter ]
He's not all that.

00:05:02.001 --> 00:05:04.603
-I mean, you can, but I would
have offered a discount.

00:05:04.603 --> 00:05:07.740
But like I said, if you're
watching, I'm available.

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.975
-Yeah, they're like,
it's 150 grand.

00:05:09.975 --> 00:05:14.613
It won't take long at all.
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:14.613 --> 00:05:18.451
-You have, you obviously
discovered some huge stars

00:05:18.451 --> 00:05:20.853
over the course of your career
doing what you've done.

00:05:20.853 --> 00:05:23.956
Yet there have been a lot of
people who have won competitions

00:05:23.956 --> 00:05:27.293
who have not had as long careers
outside the bodies of the show.

00:05:27.293 --> 00:05:29.228
Do you feel like you have
a pretty good eye

00:05:29.228 --> 00:05:31.663
when a season of one
of your shows is over of knowing

00:05:31.663 --> 00:05:35.401
who actually has the talent
for a career beyond it?

00:05:35.401 --> 00:05:36.835
-100 percent. Yeah.

00:05:36.835 --> 00:05:39.405
I mean, we always use this
expression Seth, which is,

00:05:39.405 --> 00:05:41.873
you know, forget about the show,
it's like a bubble.

00:05:41.873 --> 00:05:43.675
Outside the show
is the real world.

00:05:43.675 --> 00:05:47.446
And we try as much as we can,
as long as they're on the show,

00:05:47.446 --> 00:05:49.415
to kind of prepare them
for the real world,

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:52.452
because that's where
the real competition begins.

00:05:52.452 --> 00:05:54.253
So whether it's "Got Talent,"
you know,

00:05:54.253 --> 00:05:58.123
try to get a residency in Vegas,
the singing shows,

00:05:58.123 --> 00:06:00.425
I mean, all of the artists
you've got to compete against.

00:06:00.425 --> 00:06:03.428
So I think it's great training.

00:06:03.428 --> 00:06:05.665
But like with anything,
there's no guarantees.

00:06:05.665 --> 00:06:09.168
But luckily, over the years,
you know, we were just lucky.

00:06:09.168 --> 00:06:13.605
We met some amazing people
over the years.

00:06:13.605 --> 00:06:19.245
Normally you can tell, sometimes
in the very first audition,

00:06:19.245 --> 00:06:21.346
this person's going to have
a massive career.

00:06:21.346 --> 00:06:23.282
You can just tell,
like Carrie Underwood,

00:06:23.282 --> 00:06:25.217
I remember her first audition.

00:06:25.217 --> 00:06:27.286
It was, we weren't having
a great day

00:06:27.286 --> 00:06:28.821
and she came in and I'm like,

00:06:28.821 --> 00:06:31.523
"Wow, there's something
about this girl."

00:06:31.523 --> 00:06:34.159
And you know,
she's gone on to do great.

00:06:34.159 --> 00:06:36.729
And so, you know,
we've had some great moments.

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:39.131
We've had some not
so great moments.

00:06:39.131 --> 00:06:40.933
-Well, you've got you've had
a lot of moments.

00:06:40.933 --> 00:06:43.235
I think it's hard to have all,
be one or the other.

00:06:43.235 --> 00:06:45.471
I would imagine there's
a some sense of pride

00:06:45.471 --> 00:06:48.174
when you see those people
having these careers.

00:06:48.174 --> 00:06:49.808
I'm also wondering
what it's like you've produced

00:06:49.808 --> 00:06:52.011
so much music
over the course of your career.

00:06:52.011 --> 00:06:54.713
Do you still, does it
give you a reaction?

00:06:54.713 --> 00:06:56.081
Does give you chills
or goose bumps

00:06:56.081 --> 00:06:58.984
when you hear your music
out in the world?

00:06:58.984 --> 00:07:01.019
-Yeah. Yeah, and it's very
interesting, Seth,

00:07:01.019 --> 00:07:03.455
because our son, Eric,

00:07:03.455 --> 00:07:06.158
who's now eight,
and I'm not making this up,

00:07:06.158 --> 00:07:09.795
his favorite artist
is One Direction.

00:07:09.795 --> 00:07:13.399
So he's playing these songs now
at eight.

00:07:13.399 --> 00:07:15.535
And he's like, "Oh, Daddy
you know there's this artist

00:07:15.535 --> 00:07:17.737
called One Direction.
What do you think about?

00:07:17.737 --> 00:07:19.171
I'm like,
"Yeah, they're amazing."

00:07:19.171 --> 00:07:22.574
So it's kind of gone
full circle, but I love it.

00:07:22.574 --> 00:07:25.044
-Have you tried to jump in
and sort of explain

00:07:25.044 --> 00:07:27.046
your history
with One Direction, are you...?

00:07:27.046 --> 00:07:29.381
Because I would worry,
having kids myself,

00:07:29.381 --> 00:07:31.851
that if I told them
I'm a part of something,

00:07:31.851 --> 00:07:35.221
they would immediately not think
it was cool anymore.

00:07:35.221 --> 00:07:36.889
-You are 100 percent right.

00:07:36.889 --> 00:07:39.392
You've literally got to say
nothing.

00:07:39.392 --> 00:07:40.726
-Yeah.
-"Daddy, knew them."

00:07:40.726 --> 00:07:42.828
And that's about
as far as I've gone right now.

00:07:42.828 --> 00:07:45.964
And as he gets a bit older
I'll tell him the story.

00:07:45.964 --> 00:07:50.202
But it, it, it
it's yeah, I mean, you do.

00:07:50.202 --> 00:07:53.138
I do get a personal buzz,
particularly with him,

00:07:53.138 --> 00:07:54.940
when I hear him
playing a record.

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:57.410
And I think "Yeah, Daddy
that had the artist signed"

00:07:57.410 --> 00:08:00.379
or made the record, whatever.
Yeah, it's cool.

00:08:00.379 --> 00:08:03.983
-I very -- always fascinated
by early jobs in showbiz.

00:08:03.983 --> 00:08:06.486
You're --
Is it true that your first job

00:08:06.486 --> 00:08:09.855
was working on
the set of "The Shining"?

00:08:09.855 --> 00:08:13.392
-Well, it was interesting
because when I was about 17,

00:08:13.392 --> 00:08:16.195
I was a runner
on a. on a film set

00:08:16.195 --> 00:08:18.630
and that is like
the the bottom job,

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:23.302
you know, when you're working on
a TV show, movie, etc., etc.

00:08:23.302 --> 00:08:24.670
So I was a runner

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:27.506
and then I applied to be
a runner on "The Shining."

00:08:27.506 --> 00:08:30.242
For some reason, don't ask me
why, Stanley Kubrick

00:08:30.242 --> 00:08:32.778
would not hire runners.
I have no idea why.

00:08:32.778 --> 00:08:35.814
I mean, it was like $20
a week or something,

00:08:35.814 --> 00:08:38.917
so I got turned down.

00:08:38.917 --> 00:08:41.587
And then it happened
that Stanley Kubrick

00:08:41.587 --> 00:08:43.622
had bought my parents house,

00:08:43.622 --> 00:08:46.291
and my dad bumped into him
a few months later and said,

00:08:46.291 --> 00:08:48.527
Why didn't you give Simon
a job as a runner?

00:08:48.527 --> 00:08:50.630
He went, "Oh, I didn't know it
was Simon."

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:52.498
So he did offer me the job back.

00:08:52.498 --> 00:08:54.900
But by that point I had got a
job in the mailroom,

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:57.069
at EMI in music.

00:08:57.069 --> 00:08:59.905
So I kind of went from
possibly being

00:08:59.905 --> 00:09:02.508
in the film business into music.

00:09:02.508 --> 00:09:05.510
So it was like a, like a
sliding doors moment, almost.

00:09:05.510 --> 00:09:08.914
-So he actually did you
a great favor, it turns out.

00:09:08.914 --> 00:09:11.250
-Huge favor, yeah, thanks.

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:15.454
-I mean, that, your broken back,
a lot's gone right for you.

00:09:15.454 --> 00:09:16.822
-Silver lining.

00:09:16.822 --> 00:09:19.024
-So you mentioned
your 8-year-old

00:09:19.024 --> 00:09:21.059
and my last question
for you is,

00:09:21.059 --> 00:09:23.963
have -- has he,
has your school,

00:09:23.963 --> 00:09:25.564
have they ever reached out
and said,

00:09:25.564 --> 00:09:29.434
"Simon,
we're doing a talent show.

00:09:29.434 --> 00:09:30.735
-Yes.
-We would love to have you.

00:09:30.735 --> 00:09:32.971
Has this moment happened?

00:09:32.971 --> 00:09:35.307
-Just so happens
I'm normally out of the country.

00:09:35.307 --> 00:09:36.842
[ Laughter ]

00:09:36.842 --> 00:09:39.078
-You would, you would soften it
up for the eight year olds,

00:09:39.078 --> 00:09:41.046
wouldn't you?
-I would.

00:09:41.046 --> 00:09:43.982
But, Seth, imagine if he's
one of the people,

00:09:43.982 --> 00:09:47.453
I've got to judge, I mean,
it would be horrendous.

00:09:47.453 --> 00:09:51.790
I am really, really pushing him
to stay my side of the desk.

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:54.827
So he literally is
in training right now.

00:09:54.827 --> 00:09:57.162
-Yeah.
And I think that's good.

00:09:57.162 --> 00:09:58.997
And you're not that good a liar,
Simon.

00:09:58.997 --> 00:10:03.068
So I think you should try to --
-Thank you.

00:10:03.068 --> 00:10:04.403
-I think it's a good quality.

00:10:04.403 --> 00:10:05.671
By the way, when people
aren't good liars.

00:10:05.671 --> 00:10:07.239
It's nice to be able to know
how they're feeling.

00:10:07.239 --> 00:10:08.874
Hey, thank you so much for
making the time for us tonight.

00:10:08.874 --> 00:10:10.876
We really appreciate it.
-Seth it's been a real pleasure.

00:10:10.876 --> 00:10:13.946
-Thanks for having me on.
-Simon Cowell, everybody.

00:10:13.946 --> 00:10:17.750
"America's Got Talent Extreme"
airs Mondays at 8:00 on NBC.

